    {
      "id": 10,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        40,
        -111.82966359620633
      ],
      "size": [
        939.21875,
        315.453125
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 1,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Per-region inpainting with connectedComponentsWithStats\n\n1. This demo loads a ready-made mask image (inpaint_mask_holes.png: white spots on black) and turns it into a MASK with the core 'Convert Image to Mask' node - so it runs with no manual painting. To use your own regions instead, paint several separate areas in the MaskEditor of the top Load Image and rewire its MASK output into 'Find connected components'.\n2. 'Connected Components (Split Mask)' (cv2.connectedComponentsWithStats) splits the mask into one MASK per region and filters specks (min_area). The optional 'CV Overlay Masks' node colors the regions for inspection (delete it to skip the visualization work; bounding boxes can be drawn with the core 'Draw BBoxes' node).\n3. 'Crop by Masks' cuts a uniformly-sized, padded crop around every region (so the crops stack into one IMAGE batch) and returns the crop regions as BOUNDING_BOX ({x, y, width, height}).\n4. Each crop is inpainted - here with the fast classical 'CV Inpaint', but you can swap in any model-based inpainting on the crops/crop_masks batch.\n5. 'Paste by BBox' blends the inpainted crops back at their bounding boxes, feathered at the seams.\n\nThis crop -> process -> paste pattern works for any per-region operation, not just inpainting."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
